Run the ordinary migration and focused acceptance fixtures from test/:

    ./run.sh t-blueprint-migration t-blueprint-failures.sh t-blueprint-swap.sh

The two shell runners require Python 3; set PYTHON to its absolute executable
if the environment's python3 command is a version-manager shim. They use the
standard DRIVER and DRIVER_DEFAULTS variables, including Valgrind options.

t-blueprint-failures.sh partitions the complete request countdown across eight
isolated drivers in DEBUG plus BLUEPRINT_UPDATE_TESTING builds. Every point
through the first successful request must occur exactly once in each of the
path/explicit, path/all and loaded/explicit modes. Ordinary enabled builds run
all three successful migrations without the private controls. Disabled builds
report the conditional skip. Compiler-specific and fresh-result-copy failures
remain in t-blueprint-update's existing optional control fixtures.

t-blueprint-swap.sh tests actual program and variable swap states around
admission, deferred execution and successful publication. It omits the automatic
--check-refcounts pass because that pass unswaps objects on every backend loop.
The private native fixture checks references explicitly after swap preparation,
before publication; the LPC fixture also checks ordinary GC at each swap state.

Each worker inherits its own already-bound loopback socket. No guessed port
range or public testing efun is needed. Worker lifetimes and the overall sweep
are bounded. Signals terminate owned drivers and temporary mudlibs are removed.
The result log includes the aggregate outcome. Its .d directory retains exact
commands, fixture hashes, exits, driver logs, swap GC evidence and coverage.json.
